WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.620
It's hard to be a mad scientist, but even
harder to create an animated series that's

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.560
beloved by millions of fans around the world.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.320
We're taking a portal to the universe behind
Rick and Morty to discover little-known facts

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.380
about the show.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.280
Strap in.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.360
"Oh yeaaaah."

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.200
While the pop culture references on Rick and
Morty abound, so do allusions to the voice

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.380
actors' most famous roles.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.020
Actor and comedian David Cross plays an alien
who can't handle nudity, almost as if he's

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.400
a "never nude," like his character Tobias
on Arrested Development.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.620
"Oh I'm sorry, I need you to be completely
undressed.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.880
I am completely undressed."

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.760
Then there's Snowball, a cute dog who wants
to take over the world and is assisted by

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:45.300
an accountant.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.430
They're played by Rob Paulsen and Maurice
LaMarche, the actors who voiced the title

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:54.680
roles on Pinky and the Brain, a show about
two lab mice with designs on world domination.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.740
Actress Sarah Chalke voices Beth Smith, Rick's
daughter and Morty's mother.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.420
Beth is a horse surgeon, which makes Rick
and Morty the third time Chalke has played

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.060
a blonde doctor on TV, following her years
on Scrubs and a recurring role on How I Met

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:08.600
Your Mother.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.880
As part of the promotional blitz for the 1998
Disney movie Mulan, McDonald's briefly offered

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.460
a Szechuan dipping sauce for its Chicken McNuggets.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:24.340
In 2017, in the Rick and Morty season 3 premiere,
Rick rants that getting more of that sauce

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.220
is essentially his reason for living.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.920
"I'm driven by finding that McNugget sauce.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.950
I want that Mulan McNugget sauce, Morty.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.340
That's my series arc, Morty.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.560
If it takes nine seasons."

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.020
Consequently, the reference inspired a frenzied
response from Rick and Morty fans, leading

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.600
to petitions at Change.org to persuade McDonald's
to bring back Szechuan sauce.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.140
Leading up to the premiere of the third season
of Rick and Morty, McDonald's came through.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.400
The company posted a picture of three giant
jugs of Szechuan sauce on its Facebook page,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.100
along with an announcement that it would be
giving them all away.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.960
In addition to some lucky fans, one other
person got a very special delivery of the

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.020
sauce: Rick and Morty's own Justin Roiland.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:07.260
He tweeted out a photo of his newly acquired
stash along with a personal letter from McDonald's.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:12.860
The point of a TV show's writers' room is
to brainstorm ideas, work them out, and turn

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:13.660
them into usable stories.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.360
That's how Rick and Morty writers got the
nugget for "Pickle Rick," the famous episode

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.740
in which Rick turns himself into a pickle
and has to grapple with all kinds of challenges

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.640
to get to a family therapy appointment.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.420
Co-creator Dan Harmon told Fast Company:

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.180
"I think it was a case of somebody just saying,
'What if we started the episode this way,

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.200
with him turning himself into a pickle?'"

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:34.320
But why?

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.380
Harmon says it's because "Rick is his own
worst enemy and he'd want to prove himself."

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.480
"Stop digging for hidden layers and just be
impressed.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.469
I'm a pickle.

00:02:42.469 --> 00:02:44.680
I'm just trying to figure out why you would
do this."

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:49.559
That's when Harmon and his staff realized
they could find inspiration from a second-season

00:02:49.559 --> 00:02:54.629
episode of Breaking Bad in which Walt and
Jesse's Winnebago breaks down out in the desert.

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:55.160
Harmon explained:

00:02:55.629 --> 00:02:59.940
"We get to see Walter White up against primal
forces instead of watching him negotiate and

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.700
bluff with street dealers and kingpins.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.020
It was interesting to watch him trapped in
a hot van, about to die, and having to fall

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.540
back on his basic science knowledge."

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.200
Similarly, Rick has all sorts of tech at his
disposal, but he can rely on only his wits

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.240
to endure life as a pickle.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.100
Each episode of Rick and Morty begins with
one of the most recognizable opening sequences

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.400
in modern television.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.739
As the camera soars through space and then
transitions into scenes of Rick and Morty

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:29.080
and their family avoiding all sorts of dangers,
it's accompanied by a haunting electronic

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.040
score, driven by what sounds like synthesizers
and a theremin, a classic sci-fi combo.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.940
"I heard sci-fi noises.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.900
Did you make a breakthrough?"

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.880
Those elements sound familiar, but so does
the final product, which might be because

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.370
Rick and Morty composer Ryan Elder pulled
from three famous sci-fi theme songs in particular...although

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.520
it wasn't actually written for this show.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.520
Elder explained:

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.109
"That main theme was written for another pilot
that Justin Roiland and I were working on.

00:03:56.109 --> 00:04:00.799
It was called Dog World, and I guess the elevator
pitch would be 'a planet where dogs evolved

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.960
from men,' but it was more kid-oriented."

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.540
Roiland asked Elder to some up with a song
that was "adventurous," not unlike the themes

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:10.880
from Invader Zim, Farscape, and Doctor Who.

00:04:10.889 --> 00:04:14.599
You can't get any more direct than that, so
that's just what Elder did.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:15.280
He said:

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.420
"I kinda put all those in a blender and came
up with something that I felt had a nice adventurous

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.500
build to it, a sense of anticipation, and
forward momentum."

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.600
When they later needed a theme song for the
pilot episode of Rick &amp; Morty, Elder took

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.260
that track he'd made for Dog World and stuck
it in as a temporary fix.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:30.780
The rest is history.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:33.560
"And as time went on we just were like, you
know what?

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:34.680
Everybody likes it.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.180
Let's go with this.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.320
I barely changed it from one to the other."

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:43.340
Rick and Morty is one of Adult Swim's biggest
hits of all time, and it inspired a fanbase

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:47.690
so devoted it was willing to wage war against
a major fast food chain.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:52.040
It's a successful series by most any metric,
and yet it went on an incredibly long hiatus

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:57.230
after the final episode of season three aired
in October 2017, the first episode of season

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:01.020
four wasn't scheduled to hit the air until
November 2019, more than two years later.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:06.220
Just why and how did it take co-creators Justin
Roiland and Dan Harmon to get more episodes

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:06.920
in the can?

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:11.340
"See you for season 4 in like, a really long
time."

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.250
The simple answer: contract negotiations dragged
on.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:20.230
According to Justin Roiland in GQ, the creators
wanted enough money so Harmon wouldn't, quote,

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:24.050
"have to take 12 other jobs while we're working
on season four."

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.990
However, the wait appears to have been well
worth it.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:32.340
In 2018, Rick and Morty, a show that had only
aired 31 episodes at that point, was officially

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:38.320
renewed for a fourth season and beyond, landing
a huge 70-episode order from Adult Swim.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.960
It's not quite forever and ever, but it's
a good start.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.420
"Rick and Morty forever and forever 100 years
Rick and Morty and some things.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.020
Me and Rick and Morty running around, and
Rick and Morty time.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.320
All day long forever."

